# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datalog readiness closure — Autonomic Standing Factory case study.
#
# Datalog here is the SAME forward-chaining engine as judgment.n3,
# distinguished only by the recursive-closure + stratified-negation
# discipline these particular rules use (transitive closure over
# dependency/blocking relations, gated by negation-as-failure over
# unsatisfied dependencies) — see crates/praxis-graphlaw/src/datalog.rs
# (single stratifier/safety-checker for both .n3 files: praxis_graphlaw::
# TripleStore::load_rules parses this file into the exact same Rule/
# BodyLiteral structs as judgment.n3, and TripleStore::add_rules
# re-stratifies the COMBINED rule set on every load — so these two files
# are validated together, not as separate engines).
#
# Aggregation note: this file's .n3 text syntax has NO aggregate literal
# (praxis_graphlaw's N3/ShExC parsers do not parse aggregate expressions
# from text at all — only TripleStore::add_rule_with_aggregate, a Rust API
# call taking a Rule + Aggregate struct, can attach a Count/Sum/Min/Max/Avg
# accumulator to a rule). The unsatisfied-dependency COUNT fact is
# therefore added programmatically by src/bin/case_study_judge.rs via
# add_rule_with_aggregate, not as text here — see that file's
# `unsatisfied_dependency_count_rule` function.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@prefix praxis: <https://praxis.dev/ontology/standing#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# --- transitive closure over praxis:depends_on (mirrors this crate's own
#     {?a in ?b. ?b in ?c} => {?a in ?c} transitive-closure test pattern,
#     see crates/praxis-graphlaw/src/lib_test.rs::test_transitive_rule) ---
{ ?a praxis:depends_on ?b . ?b praxis:depends_on ?c } => { ?a praxis:depends_on ?c } .

# --- transitive closure over praxis:blocks ---
{ ?a praxis:blocks ?b . ?b praxis:blocks ?c } => { ?a praxis:blocks ?c } .

# --- a required dependency that is not (yet) satisfied is unsatisfied ---
{
  ?x praxis:requires ?dep .
  not { ?x praxis:satisfied ?dep }
} => { ?x praxis:hasUnsatisfiedDependency ?dep } .

# --- an unsatisfied dependency marked critical is a hard (terminal)
#     blocker on its requirer ---
{
  ?x praxis:hasUnsatisfiedDependency ?dep .
  ?dep praxis:critical "true"^^xsd:boolean .
} => { ?x a praxis:HasTerminalBlocker } .

# --- readiness gate: no terminal blocker => ready; a terminal blocker =>
#     not ready. Both are derived (not asserted), and disjoint by
#     stratified negation over the SAME HasTerminalBlocker relation. ---
{
  ?x a praxis:CaseStudy .
  not { ?x a praxis:HasTerminalBlocker }
} => { ?x a praxis:NoTerminalBlockers . ?x praxis:ready "true"^^xsd:boolean } .

{
  ?x a praxis:CaseStudy .
  ?x a praxis:HasTerminalBlocker .
} => { ?x praxis:not_ready "true"^^xsd:boolean } .

# --- claim promotion/demotion: any claim with >=1 evidence relation is
#     promoted; a claim recorded as promoted that turns out to have no
#     evidence at all must be demoted, never silently left standing (this
#     complements, rather than replaces, judgment.n3's hard `=> false.`
#     denial over the same shape of violation). ---
{ ?claim praxis:hasEvidence ?e } => { ?claim praxis:claim_promoted "true"^^xsd:boolean } .

{
  ?claim praxis:claim_promoted "true"^^xsd:boolean .
  not { ?claim praxis:hasEvidence ?any }
} => { ?claim praxis:claim_demoted "true"^^xsd:boolean } .

# --- external side effects: nonBlocking true is recorded as a
#     non-blocking external side effect; nonBlocking false is a hard
#     BlockingSideEffect, consumed by judgment.n3's
#     ExternalSideEffectsSeparated rule. ---
{
  ?e a praxis:ExternalOperatorSideEffect .
  ?e praxis:nonBlocking "false"^^xsd:boolean .
} => { ?e a praxis:BlockingSideEffect } .

{
  ?e a praxis:ExternalOperatorSideEffect .
  ?e praxis:nonBlocking "true"^^xsd:boolean .
} => { ?e praxis:external_side_effect "true"^^xsd:boolean . ?e praxis:non_blocking "true"^^xsd:boolean } .
